== 8. Of Marriage

Marriage is that by which the union begun in the contract is solemnly made known and brought to perfection. Marriage is completed by three sorts of actions: one of the parents of the bride and bridegroom, the second of the minister in public, the third of the persons joined together.

The action of the parents is, on the wedding day, to bring the bride and deliver her to the bridegroom, so that the two may actually become man and wife, and perform all matrimonial duties to each other.

But suppose the marriage is completed in some other manner, so that the parents, on sound judgment and deliberation, deny their full and free consent, either in express words or tacitly and by silence, and that on just and lawful cause. There, though it may stand in the civil courts of men, and the children born in it be legitimate before men, yet in truth before God it is of no force, but an utter nullity.

And because this doctrine concerning the consent of parents in these cases is of great use, and does much to support and maintain families, I will first explain what it truly means, and then prove it by reasons.

Under the name of parents are included: First, the father and mother. Secondly, all guardians who have the particular and sole charge of wards, or of others under the years of discretion. Thirdly, all who are kindred by blood and stand in the place of parents to children, such as the uncle on the father’s side, the uncle on the mother’s side, and the like.

Now as to the consent of parents, that is, of father and mother, I hold it to be required of necessity for marriage. For the authority of parents must not be resisted or violated. As for guardians, and those who have the place of parents, their consent is not required of necessity, but#footnote[De honestate.] at least of decency. For the power and authority of the parent, though it is not taken away, is yet lessened when it is either transferred to another person, or rests in part in the child already bestowed.

Secondly, by parents’ consent I mean that which they give, not rashly, unadvisedly, or foolishly, but from good and wise consideration, and on a true and sound judgment of the business in hand. For otherwise, as far as lies in them, they make the marriage void and of no effect. And they are thought to judge the matter rightly when they are able to give a just cause for giving or denying their consent. For example, if a father refuses to bestow his daughter Julia on Sempronius because he knows him to be an Arian, a Pelagian, or any other sort of heretic, he withholds his consent on a good ground, and what he does, he does from judgment.

Yet I add further that where the parents dissent and can give no reasonable cause for it, the marriage completed without their consent ought to be confirmed and ratified by the authority of the magistrate, who is #emph[Pater patriae] [the father of the country]. And this seems to be the most equitable course, both to avoid greater scandals, and to prevent the wrong that might otherwise be done in some particular case, as to a woman who has been deflowered and rejected.

Thirdly, consent of parents is either expressed or implied: expressed when it is given in words, implied when it is given by silence. For it is most equitable and impartial to think that those parents give consent who do not expressly deny it in words.

Fourthly, children are either subject to the authority of their parents in the family, or at their own liberty and out of their parents’ subjection. Those who are at liberty are not necessarily tied to subjection in respect of marriage; but the others, being still of the family and under their parents’ jurisdiction, are bound to be ordered by their parents in bestowing themselves.

This, briefly, is the meaning of the question in hand. Now, to prove this point, I will propose three sorts of arguments: some drawn from the law of God, some from the light of nature, and some from the judgment of the ancient Church.

For the first sort. According to the law of God, marriage is not only a civil and political union but also a divine and spiritual one, whose author and ordainer, for a special cause, was God himself. Our Savior Christ witnesses this when he says, “What God has joined together, let man not separate” (Matt 19:6). Therefore, so that marriages may be soundly ratified and confirmed, the authority and power of God himself must necessarily be brought in.

If it is asked, How does God, who is in heaven, bring and join together man and wife on earth?

I answer that he does it not directly by himself, as he brought Eve to Adam, but in and by some solemn and lawful means. And this means is the great and ancient power and prerogative of parents. And the law of God has given them this power, not in civil contracts and agreements alone, but even in the beginning and completing of marriages. Deut 7:3: “Do not give your daughters to their sons or take their daughters for your sons.” Jer 29:6: “Take wives for your#super[+] sons and give your#super[+] daughters in marriage, so that they too may have sons and daughters.” 1 Cor 7:36: “But if anyone thinks he is acting improperly toward his virgin daughter, if she is past the flower of her age and need so requires, let him do as he wishes; he is not sinning; they should get married.”

Hence it was that Abraham, by right of this authority, gave orders what wife his servant should provide for his son: “You will not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanites … but will go to my country and my kindred to take a wife for my son Isaac” (Gen 24:3, 4). Samson, though he was inflamed with love for the woman in Timnah, whom he saw to be beautiful in his eyes, yet did not dare take her as his wife until she was given him by his parents (Judg 14). It was an express law of God that “if a man seduces a virgin who is not pledged in marriage and sleeps with her, he must pay the full dowry for her to be his wife.” But how? Note in what manner the Holy Spirit proceeds: “If her father absolutely refuses to give her to him, the man still must pay an amount comparable to the bridal price of a virgin” (Exod 22:16, 17; Deut 22:28, 29). In this text Moses plainly teaches that the consent of the two parties is not sufficient, no, not even though they have had union together, unless they also have the free consent of their parents. In like manner it was ordained by God, Num 30:3, 4, 5, that “if a woman in her father’s house … makes a vow to the #smallcaps[Lord] … and her father hears about her vow … but says nothing to her,” that is, approves her vow by passing it over, “then all the vows … shall stand. But if her father prohibits her,” that is, gives no approval or consent to the vow made, “then none of the vows … shall stand. The #smallcaps[Lord] will absolve her because her father has not consented.” Now if the vow of children made to God, and pertaining to his worship, cannot be ratified without the parents’ approval, how much less force will any private contract or marriage have without their allowance?

The second argument is taken from the light of nature, and it is drawn by analogy in this way. A son secretly alienates and sells away his father’s lands, either in whole or in part. The question is whether this alienation is good in law or not. #emph[Answer.] No. And why? Because the land did not belong to the son, but was part of his father’s property. In like manner, a son alienates himself and is betrothed to a woman, to marry her without his parents’ knowledge. Is this act of the son warrantable and sound? By no means. For the son, in respect of his body, is part of the father’s goods, and must not be alienated from him without his consent. We find a notable reason for this in Job 1. For when God had given Satan power to destroy and spoil all Job’s goods and whatever he had, the devil touched not only Job’s wealth but his children also, as a part of his property. In this respect the Jews were permitted by God to sell their children (Exod 21:7).

Again, for marriage to be accomplished, there must be a mutual giving between the two betrothed. And what is it that is mutually given? Surely their persons, or rather their bodies, to each other; for so Paul says, 1 Cor 7:2: “each man should have his own wife, and each woman her own husband.” But by whom is this giving to be made? By sons and daughters who are in the family, under the jurisdiction of their parents? It cannot be. For nature herself takes it for granted that he who is not at his own liberty cannot consent to the giving of himself. The giving therefore remains in the right of the parent, inasmuch as the will and consent of the child ought to depend on the will and consent of him to whom God has given power and authority in this matter.

In the third place, let the judgment of the ancient Church be observed. Ambrose, in his first book on the patriarch Abraham, ch. 9, requires this consent in marriages, and says it is so just and agreeable to nature that even the poets acknowledged it. For this purpose he recites two verses from the Greek poet Euripides, in his tragedy called #emph[Andromache.] There, when Orestes desired to marry Hermione, she gives him this answer: that the matter of her marriage depended wholly on the pleasure and authority of her father, and was not in her own power or liberty. Innocent, who was both a pope and a martyr, in his decretal epistles so strictly requires the consent of parents#footnote[Sub Innocent. 3. canon. 51.] that “where it is wanting, he concludes the marriage to be void.” In the Council of Lateran it was decreed that clandestine contracts and “secret marriages, made and undertaken between party and party, should be held as unlawful as marriages in those degrees of consanguinity and affinity which are forbidden by God.” Tertullian says#footnote[Tertul. lib. ad vxorem, 2. ad finem.] that “in this world the son cannot#footnote[Ritè & Iure. Basil. epist. 1. ad Amphiloch. Canon, 42.] rightly and lawfully marry without the allowance of his father.” Basil, in an epistle to Amphilochius, bishop of Iconium, affirms “that marriages which are made without those who have power over the parties are fornications; and therefore that those who are joined together in marriage while their parents or masters live are in no way free from the charge of sin until they have approval from them; for then marriage is ratified and confirmed, when they have given their allowance to it.”

The second action in completing marriage is the action of the minister. And that is the blessing or sanctification of it: a solemn work by which the minister, pronouncing the contracted parties to be man and wife before the whole congregation, commends them and their state to God by solemn prayer.

This solemn sanctification is grounded on the practice of God himself, who, having given Eve to Adam, blessed them, saying, “Be fruitful and multiply” (Gen 2:22; 1:28). For what God then gave, the minister, standing in his place, now prays may be given to the man and wife. Indeed, it agrees with the general rule of the apostle, “Everything must be done in a proper and orderly manner” (1 Cor 14:40).

Now that this action is to be approved and used in the Church appears from these reasons.

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ Marriage, as it is a public action, so it is in a sense a spiritual and divine ordinance, and in this it differs from the contract. For the contract, being merely civil, as it stands by the consent of men, so by the same consent it may be broken and dissolved; but with marriage it is otherwise.
+ Marriage is the seedbed of the Church and commonwealth.
+ It was the practice of the early Church. Tertullian, speaking of the marriage of Christians with Christians,#footnote[Lib. 2. ad vxorem.] acknowledges that he is not able sufficiently to declare the happiness “of that union which is made by the Church, consecrated by prayers and#footnote[Oblationem sic exponit Beatus Rhenanus [So Beatus Rhenanus explains “oblation”]. Niceph. lib. 14. cap. 55. Ambros. epistolar. lib. 9. epist. 70. ad Vigilium.] solemn service, witnessed by the angels, and ratified by God himself.” Nicephorus reports of one Synesius, who declared that “God and the laws, and the holy hand of Theophilus, had given him a wife.” Ambrose says “that marriage ought to be sanctified with the veil with which the priest used to cover the bride and the bridegroom in the public congregation, and with his blessing.”

The third and last action belonging to the completing of this state is that of the parties themselves, by which the bride is brought, in a decent and modest manner, to the house and home of the bridegroom. It is the law of this state, published by God himself in Paradise, that the man, even in respect of where he dwells, must “leave his father and mother and be united to his wife” (Gen 2:24).

Here the question is raised whether marriage is to be solemnized with mirth and feasting. #emph[Answer.] I. It is lawful and warrantable to use feasting and mirth at marriages, because these are things neither commanded nor forbidden, and we have examples of them in the Scriptures. Laban made a feast at the wedding of Jacob and Rachel, and invited all the men of the place to it (Gen 29:22). Christ himself approved the gathering of people at the marriage at Cana in Galilee, both by his presence and by that honorable gift of six water jars of the best wine (John 2:2, 7, 8). II. It is not only lawful, but fitting and proper to be done, if there is ability, according to the commendable custom of the place and country where men live, provided that in its use these cautions are observed. First, that in mirth and merrymaking care be taken that nothing is done that is dishonorable, profane, or of ill report. Phil 4:8: “Whatever is honorable, … whatever is pure, … whatever is of good report … think on these things.” Secondly, that joy in them be mixed and tempered with the fear of God, without which laughter and rejoicing is sheer madness (Eccl 2:2). Thirdly, that it be done with a moderate and sober use of food and drink, without revelry and excess. So we read that at the great feast of Ahasuerus it was appointed by the king himself that they should drink in an orderly way, and none might compel another to drink more than he thought fitting (Esth 1:8). And where weddings are kept contrary to these directions, they are not feasts celebrated to God, but to the devil; which may also be said of all other festive gatherings.

And so much for the point of marriage. In it this is to be remembered as a conclusion: that where there is in general a nullity in the contract, or a separation following on it, there is also a nullity in regard to the completing of marriage.
